=== CPU and Memory Sizing

Concurrency parameters scale together and should be sized against available CPU cores and the database connection pool, not set independently:

- `pull.thread.per.server.count`, `push.thread.per.server.count`, and `routing.thread.per.server.count` each spawn that many concurrent worker threads per server. Every additional thread is a candidate for a concurrent database connection and a share of CPU time, so size these to the CPU cores available to the JVM, not to the number of nodes being synchronized.
- `http.concurrent.workers.max` bounds concurrent inbound push/pull HTTP requests per servlet. Its own documentation recommends keeping `db.pool.max.active` at least twice `http.concurrent.workers.max` plus roughly 10 connections of headroom, so request concurrency never starves the pool.
- For high-throughput sync workloads, set `db.pool.min.idle` equal to `db.pool.max.active`, trading idle-connection overhead for eliminating connection-creation latency during bursts. Apache DBCP2 is the default pool (`db.pool.type`); this specific guidance comes from HikariCP's own documentation, so it applies most directly when `db.pool.type=hikari` is set to opt into HikariCP instead — a fully supported, dependency-free alternative already bundled with the distribution.
- There is no universal JVM heap preset. Heap needs scale with channel count and batch size, and are already partly self-protecting via `routing.peek.ahead.memory.threshold.percent`, which caps how much of the heap the routing reader's peek-ahead queue may consume before it disperses non-active transactions to avoid an out-of-memory error. Rather than guessing a static `-Xmx`, enable the `cpu`, `memory`, `loadAverage`, `connectionPool`, `fileHandles`, and `jvmOutOfMemory` <<Monitors>>, size heap and cores from what they show under real load, and re-check after any change to thread-count or batch-size parameters.

=== Staging Area Placement and Sizing

The <<Staging Area>> writes extraction and load payloads to disk once `stream.to.file.threshold.bytes` is exceeded, specifically to stop large syncs from holding a database connection or JVM heap open for the duration of a network transfer.

In a clustered deployment (see <<Clustering>>), placing staging on a shared network drive rather than local disk is the better default: with `staging.dir` pointed at the share and `cluster.staging.enabled` turned on, a batch extracted in the background by one node can be served by any node, instead of every node re-extracting the same batch. This is the same mechanism that lets local staging be disabled during initial load (`initial.load.use.extract.job.enabled=false`) once shared staging is available.

The trade-off: shared storage now sits directly in the sync data path, so its latency and throughput matter as much as the database's. A share provisioned on an archival or cold-storage tier trades one bottleneck (database connection pressure) for another (staging I/O wait). Provision a low-latency, high-IOPS tier — for example EFS with provisioned throughput on AWS, a premium-tier Azure Files share, or a high-scale Filestore instance on Google Cloud — sized for peak concurrent batch volume, not average. Watch the `disk` monitor against the staging volume the same way the database connection pool is watched, since a full staging volume fails syncs the same way a full database does.

=== Clustering and Networking

See <<Firewall and Network Requirements>> for the TCP, UDP, and HTTP(S) ports used between cluster nodes and from the load balancer. Peer discovery uses two independent paths, and a node only needs one of them working: Apache JCS's own UDP multicast (fast, but only where the network permits IP multicast), and a database-driven path that announces every node's address from `SYM_NODE_HOST` directly to JCS (works over plain TCP anywhere, but bounded by the heartbeat job's cadence — see the speed comparison below). The notes below cover which path is actually available in each environment.

Group of Servers or VMs (on-premises or bare-metal LAN):: Multicast works normally as long as every node sits on one Layer 2 segment (VLAN/subnet), giving the fast discovery path. Watch for switches with IGMP snooping enabled but no IGMP querier configured — a common, silent cause of multicast packets being dropped even though port-level firewall rules are correct. When that happens, clustering still works; discovery just falls back to the slower `SYM_NODE_HOST` cadence below.

Local Docker Compose:: Gets the fast discovery path out of the box on a single Docker host: a user-defined bridge network floods multicast to every attached container, the same way an unmanaged Ethernet switch would. In a working three-node setup, all three SymmetricDS containers share one bridge network and never publish 1101/tcp or 6789/udp to the host, because they only need to reach each other, not the host. Multi-host Docker (Swarm overlay networks) loses the fast path for the same reason Kubernetes does, below — clustering still works, just at the slower cadence.

AWS (ECS, EKS, Fargate):: AWS VPCs do not support multicast or broadcast traffic, regardless of subnet or security group configuration — a platform limitation, not something firewall rules can fix. JCS's UDP discovery cannot find peers across ENIs or hosts, so clustering here always runs on the slower `SYM_NODE_HOST` path. The only way to get the fast path is a Transit Gateway multicast domain, which supports EC2 instances but not Fargate tasks — worth it only if fast convergence after scaling events matters more than the added setup.

Azure Kubernetes Service:: Azure VNETs and AKS's supported CNI plugins (Azure CNI, kubenet) do not forward multicast between pods, so clustering runs on the slower path here too. Some CNI/overlay combinations are sometimes reported to support multicast, but it is not part of AKS's supported network model — do not depend on it without validating the exact CNI and version in use.

Google Kubernetes Engine:: Google Cloud documents VPC multicast and broadcast as unsupported, with no workaround at the network layer. Same outcome as AWS and AKS: always the slower `SYM_NODE_HOST` path.

Oracle Cloud Infrastructure (OCI/OKE):: OCI VCNs explicitly disallow the Class D address range (224.0.0.0-239.255.255.255) used for multicast — it is rejected outright, not merely unrouted. Oracle's own guidance for clustered software on OCI is to switch from multicast to unicast peer discovery, which is effectively what the `SYM_NODE_HOST` path already does here automatically.

Akamai Cloud (Linode/LKE):: Standard LKE clusters cannot join a VPC at all (VPC support is limited to LKE-Enterprise), so this inherits the same pod-to-pod multicast gap as the managed Kubernetes offerings above. The one exception in this survey: a non-containerized deployment directly on Linode VMs using their legacy private networking — a real Layer 2 segment, distinct from the newer VPC product — should support multicast normally, the same as the bare-metal/VM case above.

[NOTE]
.Discovery speed: UDP multicast vs. the SYM_NODE_HOST path
====
Every node announces itself to JCS from `SYM_NODE_HOST` in addition to (not instead of) UDP multicast, so clustering no longer depends on multicast routing — it only affects how fast a new peer is found. UDP multicast converges in roughly 15 seconds (JCS's fixed passive-broadcast interval). The `SYM_NODE_HOST` path is bounded by the heartbeat job instead: a node discovers all of its peers immediately at its own startup, but an *already-running* node only learns about a newly-joined peer at its own next heartbeat tick — `job.heartbeat.period.time.ms`, 15 minutes by default. In any environment above that lacks working multicast (every managed Kubernetes offering, or a LAN with misconfigured IGMP snooping), lower `job.heartbeat.period.time.ms` if fast convergence after scaling events matters more than the added heartbeat query load; leave it at the default for a stable cluster where convergence time isn't operationally significant.
====

=== Security Hardening

- Disable `auto.registration` once all expected nodes have been provisioned. Leaving registration open in production lets any client that can reach the sync URL register as a node.
- `cluster.jcs.port` carries plain serialized Java objects with no transport-level encryption or authentication, per its own parameter description. The `sym.secret`-based AES encryption described in <<Clustering>> protects the message payload, but the raw socket itself is unauthenticated, so the port must stay on a private, firewalled subnet exactly as <<Firewall and Network Requirements>> describes — the same applies to the UDP discovery port.
- Never reuse example credentials from a lab or quick-start environment. Treat hardcoded database and keystore-seed values, like the ones in the local Docker Compose lab, as strictly non-production.
- Grant the SymmetricDS database user only the privileges it needs against the schemas it synchronizes; avoid handing it broader account privileges than the sync workload requires.
- Enforce HTTPS end-to-end. If TLS terminates at the load balancer rather than at each node, confirm the load-balancer-to-node hop is also encrypted, or is on a network segment trusted to the same degree as the one required for `cluster.jcs.port`.
